course: technology
lesson: 30
title: Bridges and skyscrapers
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Every bridge and every building has one job: to carry its own weight, and the weight of everything on it, safely down into the ground. Engineers choose a shape that does this best for each place.
explain: A beam bridge is a plank resting on supports. An arch pushes its weight sideways and down into the ground at its ends, so its stones are squeezed together and hold each other tight. A suspension bridge hangs its road from huge cables slung over tall towers.
explain: Lie a ruler across two books and press the middle: it bends. Curve a strip of card into an arch between the books, with its ends held against them, and it carries far more. Shape, not just material, makes things strong.
explain: A skyscraper is a strong steel or concrete core and frame standing on deep foundations; its glass walls are only a skin. Tall buildings even sway a little in the wind on purpose, because a building that can bend a little does not snap.
fact: A suspension bridge hangs its road from smaller cables attached to huge main cables, which run over tall towers and are anchored in rock or concrete at the ends.
fact: The Golden Gate Bridge's main cables contain enough wire to wrap around the Earth more than three times.
fact: A beam bridge rests on supports, while an arch pushes the weight sideways and down into the ground; Roman stone arch bridges still stand after about 2,000 years.
fact: Lifting and swing bridges use motors and counterweights to lift or turn so tall ships can pass; Tower Bridge in London has opened for ships since 1894. Never stand on a bridge that is about to open.
fact: Bridges grow longer in heat and shorter in cold, and expansion joints, metal teeth or gaps, let them move, since without them a bridge swelling in the heat would push hard against its ends and could crack or buckle; long bridges can change length by tens of centimetres between winter and summer.
fact: A skyscraper's strong steel or concrete core and frame carry the weight down to deep foundations; the walls are just a skin. The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, over 800 metres tall, is the tallest building in the world for now.
fact: Tall buildings bend a little in the wind; some have huge weights near the top that swing to calm the movement, and Taipei 101 has a giant golden ball weighing over 600 tonnes.
fact: Skyscraper windows are cleaned from a cradle that hangs from rails on the roof and moves down the building, and some buildings use robot cleaners.
fact: Earthquake-proof buildings can stand on rubber and steel pads or springs, so the ground shakes while the building moves less; in an earthquake, drop, cover and hold on.
fact: Scaffolding is a temporary frame of steel poles and boards clamped together by hand by scaffolders, with diagonal braces so it does not wobble; never climb scaffolding.
fact: A house's foundations are concrete under the walls that spread its weight over the ground, so it does not sink; houses on clay can move a little in very dry summers, because the clay shrinks.
fact: A suspension bridge usually has 2 tall towers.
exercise: How can cables hold up a whole road on a suspension bridge? | The road hangs from cables slung over tall towers and anchored at the ends | The cables are stiff like steel poles under the road | The road floats on the air between the towers | The cables pull the road up using motors
exercise: How much wire is in the Golden Gate Bridge's main cables? | Enough to wrap around the Earth more than three times | Enough to cross the bridge once, from one end to the other | About as much as a fishing line | Enough to reach the Moon and back
exercise: Why is an arch so strong? | It pushes the weight sideways and down, squeezing its stones together | Its stones are glued with very strong glue | The middle stone is made of iron | The air under the arch holds it up
exercise: How long have some Roman stone arch bridges stood? | About 2,000 years | About 20 years | About 200 years | About 20,000 years
exercise: How does Tower Bridge let tall ships pass? | Its road lifts up, helped by counterweights | It sinks down under the water for each ship to pass | The ships fold their tall masts down flat to fit | The whole bridge slides sideways on big wheels
exercise: Since when has Tower Bridge opened for ships? | 1894 | 1994 | 1794 | 1854
exercise: Why do bridges have metal gaps or teeth called expansion joints? | So the bridge can grow longer in heat and shorter in cold | So rain can drain off the road | So cars slow down when they get onto the bridge and keep a safe gap | So the bridge can be folded in winter
exercise: What holds up a skyscraper? | A strong core and frame carrying the weight to deep foundations | Its thick glass walls, which are stronger than steel and hold up every floor | Air pressure inside the building | Ropes tied to the clouds
exercise: How tall is the Burj Khalifa in Dubai? | Over 800 metres | About 80 metres, like a block of flats | About 8 kilometres | About 300 metres
exercise: Do skyscrapers move in the wind? | Yes, they bend a little, and some have giant weights to calm it | No, they are completely rigid, like a rock that never moves at all | Only when they are about to fall | Only the windows move, not the building
exercise: What does Taipei 101 have near its top to steady it? | A giant golden ball weighing over 600 tonnes | A huge sail at the top to catch the strong wind | A swimming pool full of sea water to weigh it down | A helicopter tied on top that pulls it straight
exercise: How are skyscraper windows cleaned? | From a cradle hanging from rails on the roof, or by robots | By firefighters spraying water up from the street below with long hoses | They are never cleaned | By the rain alone, because glass cleans itself
exercise: How can a building survive an earthquake? | By standing on rubber and steel pads that let the ground shake under it | By being made of very thin glass | By having no foundations at all | By being painted with a special paint
exercise: What should you do in an earthquake? | Drop, cover and hold on | Run outside past falling glass | Stand in a lift until it stops | Climb up the stairs to the roof
exercise: Why isn't scaffolding wobbly? | Its poles are clamped together with diagonal braces | It is glued to the wall of the house with strong builders' glue all the way up | It is filled with sand | It is held up by the workers
exercise: What stops a house sinking into the ground? | Concrete foundations spread its weight over the ground | Its roof pulls it upwards | The grass roots hold it up | Its water pipes push it up from below like the legs of a table
exercise: Count them: a river has 3 suspension bridges, each with 2 towers. How many towers? | 6 | 5 | 3 | 9
test: «lol bridges don't move, they're solid» What is true? | Bridges grow and shrink with heat and cold, by tens of centimetres on long ones | True, steel never changes size | Only wooden bridges move | They move only when a ship hits them
test: «my bro says the glass walls hold the skyscraper up» What is right? | No, the core and frame hold it up; the glass is only a skin | Yes, very thick glass holds it up | Yes, and that is why the windows in skyscrapers can never be opened | No, it is held up by the lifts inside
test: «are you thick? if a skyscraper sways it's about to fall» What is right? | No, tall buildings are built to bend a little in the wind | Yes, any sway at all means the building is cracking and will fall down soon | Yes, so everyone must leave at once | No, skyscrapers never move at all
test: What would happen to a long bridge with no expansion joints on a very hot summer day? | It would push hard against its ends and could crack or buckle | Nothing, bridges don't change size | It would get shorter in the heat, slide off the ends and fall off its supports | It would float up off its supports
test: A friend wants to climb the scaffolding on the empty house next door for a view. What is right? | Never climb scaffolding; it is for trained workers | Fine, if it isn't wobbly | Fine, if nobody is working on it that day and the ladder is left out | Fine, if you only go one floor up
test: In a cartoon, a huge earthquake hits and the hero runs out of the building past falling glass. What does the safety rule say instead? | Drop, cover and hold on | Run outside as fast as you can | Take the lift to the ground floor | Stand by the windows to watch
test: Why does the Taipei 101 golden ball help in strong wind? | It swings against the building's sway and calms it | It makes the building lighter at the top, so the wind can't push it | It catches the wind like a sail | It is only there as a gold store
test: Two piles of the same stones: one built as an arch, one as a flat beam laid across the gap with no support in the middle. Which carries more weight? | The arch, because it pushes the weight down into its ends | The flat beam, because it has more stone on top and the weight sits straight down | Both carry exactly the same | Neither can carry any weight
test: «the house cracked in the drought, how? there's no earthquake» What is a likely reason? | It stands on clay, which shrinks in very dry summers and moves the foundations | Drought makes bricks melt | The heat makes houses grow taller | Dry air pushes on the walls
test: «Tower Bridge is going up and I'm still on it, sod it, I'll just hold on» What is right? | Never stand on a bridge that is about to open: leave it when told | Holding on is fine, it lifts slowly and gently, so you won't slide off the road | It's fun, people do it every day | Only cars must get off, not people
test: A window-cleaning cradle cleans 4 floors an hour. How many hours for a 32-floor building? | 8 | 28 | 36 | 128
test: Which shows the bridge facts correctly? | Suspension bridges hang the road; arches push weight into their ends | Suspension bridges push weight into their ends; arches hang the road | Both kinds just rest on a plank | Both kinds float on the water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which an arch sustains a load? | It transmits the load sideways and downwards into its abutments, so that its stones are compressed together | Its stones are bonded by adhesive of exceptional strength | Its keystone alone, being of iron, sustains the load | The air beneath the arch supports it from below
exam: Identify the principal function of expansion joints upon a bridge. | They permit the structure to lengthen in heat and contract in cold without damage | They facilitate the drainage of rainwater from the carriageway | They compel motorists to reduce their speed | They permit the bridge to be folded during winter
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the structural support of a skyscraper. | A core and frame of steel or concrete convey its weight to deep foundations, the external walls being a mere skin | The glazed external walls sustain the entire weight of the structure | The structure is sustained by the air pressure within | The structure is sustained by its lifts and staircases alone
exam: It is given that an earthquake has commenced. Ascertain the conduct recommended to a person within a building. | To drop to the floor, to take cover and to hold on | To run outside forthwith past the windows into the open street | To descend by means of a lift | To ascend to the roof of the building
